35,2,1,talk,Rin,,"It turns out the morning routine upstairs takes a while—between four and nine minutes just to get set up! Also, the badge records show we arrive at totally different times.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The floor billing is based on area, not headcount, meaning we pay for empty desks. Also, re-running power and network costs 6,200 yen per desk every time we change.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"The staff survey shows daylight is a huge complaint, but the current plan only gives four people windows. Also, the new joiners said they learned a lot just by overhearing us.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shion,,"The sound levels are significantly higher by the printer and door, and the physio noted that it takes days of small adjustments to get a workstation's ergonomics right.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Yumi,,"I found out that when project strands sat together last spring, they barely needed written notes because they just talked! But unplanned shuffles were a nightmare, taking two weeks of chaos.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Rin,,"@Toshio, if it costs that much to move power and network every time, maybe we shouldn't change the layout too often? That sounds like a lot of money!","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"It is a significant recurring cost. If we choose a system that requires frequent changes, we will be draining a budget that is already quite tight.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"That budget concern is important, but we also can't ignore the daylight issue. If only four of us get windows, we need a fair way to decide who sits where.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shion,,"The physio's note is the real kicker here. If it takes days to settle into a workstation, any system that forces us to move frequently will be a physical disaster.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Yumi,,"But the project strand thing was so great! If we sit together, we save so much time on notes. Maybe there's a way to stay together without the chaos of shuffles?","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Rin,,"Wait, if we arrive at different times, a first-come system would be totally unfair! Some of us would always get the best spots before others even get here.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"I agree. Combined with the cost of re-running power, a system that encourages constant movement or random selection seems inefficient and financially risky.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"It seems we are caught between the need for fairness in lighting and the efficiency of communication, while also facing these physical and financial constraints.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shion,,"Exactly. If we move too much, we lose our ergonomic settings and waste money; if we don't move, some people are stuck in the noise or the dark.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Yumi,,"Maybe we can find a middle ground? I really want us to keep that easy communication, but I don't want anyone to be unhappy with their spot or stressed by the chaos!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Rin,,"I'm starting to think that moving every day or every week is a bad idea, especially with that setup time and the arrival gaps!","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Toshio,,I concur. The financial burden of frequent re-wiring and the operational loss of setup time make a highly fluid arrangement impractical for our current budget and schedule.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"We also have to consider the joiners' experience; if we separate too much, we lose that organic learning that happens when we're within earshot of each other.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shion,,"If we prioritize ergonomics and noise control, we need stability. Constant shifting would mean we never actually get comfortable in our seats, which is a price I'm not willing to pay.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Yumi,,"I agree with Shion! Stability sounds like the best way to avoid that two-week chaos I found in the shuffle records, while still letting us stay close to each other.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
